# Release Notes Rules

- Production release notes live in `docs/release-notes/`.
- When preparing a repository GitHub Release, write the release note section as the final publish-ready body. Do not label it as a draft, suggested draft, or temporary copy.
- Use the heading `## GitHub Release Body` for the text that should be pasted into the GitHub Release description.
- Keep the body self-contained: summarize user-visible changes, important fixes, artifact names, checksums when useful, and operational notes needed by users of the packaged build.
- Avoid including process-only wording such as "draft", "suggested", "copy later", or "to be edited before publishing" unless the user explicitly asks for an internal planning note.

- Public GitHub Release bodies use English, following v1.5.8: a short user-facing opening paragraph, `### What's Changed` with bold feature groups, `### Upgrade Notes` (or Getting Started when appropriate), `### Downloads` with direct asset links, and `### Full Changelog` with the previous published release comparison. Keep Chinese implementation/validation records in internal docs, not the public release body.
- Apply the same style to Full, Server and Desktop releases. Describe component versions and actual upgrade boundaries; omit worktree/merge history, unpublished candidate debugging and build-process narration.
- Component note files use `<component>-vX.Y.Z.md`. Their `## GitHub Release Body` section is extracted for publishing; document titles stay outside the GitHub description.
